Wire member type exprs through algebra/

`walk_if_atom`'s `IfAtom::Var` branch was previously skipping member
type annotations: `resolve_type_expr` returned `None` for
`TypeExpr::Member`, so the annotated el ended up with no concrete
type. Now `walk_var_type_expr` resolves all three type-expr kinds,
and for `e.Name` it walks `e`, looks up `Name` in `e`'s model body
via `member_scope_and_parents`, and builds a `ConcreteType` whose
parent chain is `e`'s parents with `e` itself appended.

Annotated types now flow through a new
`Structure::pending_type_impositions` queue rather than being
written directly to `els`. Populate enqueues an `(el, ct)` pair via
`Structure::impose_type`, and `Structure::close` applies them all
once equality, functionality and signature-derived typing have
settled. A var-if-atom annotation that disagrees with an inferred
type is therefore reported with the inferred type as `a` and the
annotation as `b`. Idempotency is preserved across the populate/close
fixed-point loop by canonicalising and de-duplicating entries on
push, and `canonicalise_refs` rewrites pending impositions to current
roots.

A regression test `conflicting-term-type-member-annotation` exercises
the new branch: a member-typed func argument forces one type onto a
variable whose annotation asserts a different member type.
